  the server dispatches one core's statements on one thread, so
  a rising aggregate here is round-trip overlap, not parallel
  execution. Where it stops rising is where the client stopped
  being the bottleneck.

plans (ANALYZE)
statement             steps                                   rows    examined
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
backtest-read         FilterScan+Probe+Probe                     4       1,016
compare-all           Scan+Scan                                 88         173
compare-one           Lookup+FilterScan                         11          89
read-bar-lookup       Lookup                                     1           1
read-bar-range        Range                                    200         261
read-day-slice        FilterScan                                 4       1,008

  a Probe or Lookup step on a HEAP relation has no index to
  descend and walks the chain instead: `examined` far above
  `rows` is that, and is what --bars-clustered heap measures.
  read-bar-lookup is the clean read of it. On the *joins* it can
  be hidden: these statements have run hundreds of times by now,
  so their lookup-class steps may be served from a Waystone trail
  (docs/spec/waystone-concpets.md) - a recorded (page, slot) instead
  of a search, which is exactly what a trail is for and is why an
  ANALYZE taken after a warm run is not a cold plan.

